data.content:
Here's the complete migration. Your code has three patterns from the skill guide:

| Pattern | Location |
|---------|----------|
| **Cancellable work loop** | `while (_running)` in `ProcessLoop` |
| **ThreadAbortException as control flow** | `catch` block with `RollbackCurrentTransaction()` |
| **Thread.ResetAbort** | Preventing rethrow to keep thread alive |
| **Thread.Abort** | `Stop()` force-killing the thread |

---

### Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        var token = _cts.Token;
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Cancellation requested — clean up partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — we control the loop.
                // Loop condition will exit naturally.
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait up to 2 seconds for graceful shutdown
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }
}
```

---

### What Changed and Why

| Before (.NET Framework) | After (.NET 8) | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| `volatile bool _running` | `CancellationToken` | Token integrates with blocking APIs; a bool cannot wake a blocked thread |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` | `ThreadAbortException` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+ |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Removed | Cooperative cancellation doesn't auto-rethrow — the loop simply continues or exits |
| `Thread.Abort()` | `_cts.Cancel()` | Signals all token holders cooperatively |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` + abort | `_workerThread.Join(2s)` | Waits for the thread to finish gracefully with a timeout |

---

### Critical Pitfall: `GetNextItem()` Must Accept the Token

If `GetNextItem()` blocks (e.g., on a queue or I/O), **cancellation stalls there** unless you pass the token through. Example fix:

```csharp
// Before: blocks forever, ignores cancellation
private Item GetNextItem()
{
    return _queue.Take(); // BlockingCollection.Take()
}

// After: wakes immediately on cancellation
private Item GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    return _queue.Take(token); // throws OperationCanceledException
}
```

If using `Thread.Sleep` anywhere in the work path, replace with:
```csharp
// Before:
Thread.Sleep(5000);

// After (cancellation-aware):
token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(5000);
```

### Other Pitfalls to Watch

1. **Dispose the `CancellationTokenSource`** — it's `IDisposable`. Leaking it causes timer/callback leaks. The example above uses `IDisposable` on the class itself.
2. **Don't swallow `OperationCanceledException`** deeper in the call chain — only catch it at this top-level loop. The `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` filter ensures you don't accidentally catch unrelated OCEs.
3. **Cancellation is not immediate** — it only fires at the next checkpoint (`token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` or a token-aware API call). If `ProcessItem` is long-running, add checkpoints inside it:
   ```csharp
   private void ProcessItem(Item item, CancellationToken token)
   {
       DoStepOne(item);
       token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
       DoStepTwo(item);
       token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
       DoStepThree(item);
   }
   ```
